// Title: Unusual Process Communication with Public Ethereum RPC Infrastructure
//
// Description:
// Threat-hunting query for identifying endpoint processes communicating with public Ethereum
// JSON-RPC infrastructure. This behavior can be used legitimately by browsers, wallets,
// developer tooling, and security products, but it is also relevant to GoCaracal's documented
// fallback C2-resilience mechanism, which queries Ethereum smart-contract storage for a
// replacement command-and-control address after repeated primary C2 failures.
//
// Classification: Accepted — Hunting Query
// This is intentionally not a production detection rule. Process-to-RPC relationships are
// environment-specific and require analyst validation.
//
// MITRE ATT&CK:
// T1102.003 - Web Service: One-Way Communication
// T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols
//
// False-positive / baseline guidance:
// Expected activity may include browsers, cryptocurrency wallets, blockchain applications,
// developer tooling, automation, and security products. Prioritize rare or previously unseen
// standalone executables, unusual user-writable paths, and processes with no business reason
// to access public Ethereum infrastructure.
//
// Investigation guidance:
// - Validate the process signer, prevalence, path, command line, parent, and SHA256.
// - Determine whether the device/user has a legitimate blockchain-related use case.
// - Review nearby network activity for failed primary C2 connections followed by new outbound
//   infrastructure shortly after the Ethereum RPC access.
// - Review process/file activity for additional payload execution, persistence, credential theft,
//   or remote-shell behavior.
// - If HTTP-layer telemetry is available elsewhere, look specifically for Ethereum JSON-RPC
//   methods such as eth_getStorageAt.
//
// Source Article:
// Arctic Wolf Labs - Dark Caracal Reloaded: New Malware, Same Hunting Grounds
// https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/dark-caracal-reloaded-new-malware-same-hunting-grounds/

let Lookback = 30d;
let EthereumRpcDomains = dynamic([
    "infura.io",
    "alchemy.com",
    "ankr.com",
    "cloudflare-eth.com",
    "ethereum.publicnode.com",
    "eth.llamarpc.com"
]);
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where Timestamp >= ago(Lookback)
| where RemoteUrl has_any (EthereumRpcDomains)
| where isnotempty(InitiatingProcessFileName)
| summarize
    Events = count(),
    Devices = dcount(DeviceName),
    FirstSeen = min(Timestamp),
    LastSeen = max(Timestamp),
    RemoteIPs = make_set(RemoteIP, 10),
    RemoteUrls = make_set(RemoteUrl, 10),
    SampleCommandLines = make_set(InitiatingProcessCommandLine, 5)
    by
        InitiatingProcessFileName,
        InitiatingProcessFolderPath
| order by Devices asc, Events asc
